GPUI makes two textures the size of the window for drawing paths the first
time a window draws anything at all: one to rasterize into, and an MSAA one
four times its size to resolve from. It makes them whether the scene has a
path in it or not, and keeps them for as long as the window lives.

For a shell that is a lot of video memory spent on nothing. Most of its
windows have no path in them, and some are the size of a screen — the lock,
the screenshot picker — where the two come to forty-six megabytes a screen.

So they are made the first time a frame has a path to draw. A frame without
one never looks at them, and the code that draws paths already copes with
their not being there.

--- a/crates/gpui_wgpu/src/wgpu_renderer.rs
+++ b/crates/gpui_wgpu/src/wgpu_renderer.rs
@@ -1338,8 +1331,14 @@ impl WgpuRenderer {
             }
         };
 
-        // Now that we know the surface is healthy, ensure intermediate textures exist
-        self.ensure_intermediate_textures();
+        // Now that we know the surface is healthy, ensure intermediate textures
+        // exist — if anything is going to be drawn into them. They are the
+        // size of the window, and the MSAA one four times over: for a window
+        // the size of a screen that is tens of megabytes of video memory, kept
+        // for as long as the window lives, for a scene with no paths in it.
+        if !scene.paths.is_empty() {
+            self.ensure_intermediate_textures();
+        }
 
         let frame_view = frame
             .texture
